        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Thessaloniki</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 315 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">Around 315 BCE by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, on or near the site of the ancient town of Therma and 26 other local villages; he named it after his wife Thessalonike, a half-sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a>, at the north-west corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, bounded on the west by the delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a>, in Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">A city on the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a>'s palace, arch and mausoleum; St Demetrius; the second city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>; the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zealots">Zealots</a> of 1342; the largest Sephardic Jewish community; the Young Turk Revolution of 1908; the Great Fire of 1917</span></div>
