    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> founded the city around 315 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> made it a provincial capital on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> and later emperors it became the second city of the Byzantine world after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1430, and after 1492 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> settled there, so that by 1519 they made up 54% of the city's population.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> took the city in 1912, the fire of 1917 destroyed its centre, the exchange of populations changed its makeup, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of 1943 destroyed its Jewish community.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1519, according to Ottoman archives, Thessaloniki had 15,715 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, 6,870 Muslims and about 6,635 Christians, and the Jews were 54% of the city's population.</p></aside>
    </section>

