    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 315 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> founds the city and names it after his wife Thessalonike</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">306</span><span class="rf-text">St Demetrius becomes the patron saint of the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">904</span><span class="rf-text">A naval force led by Leo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripoli</a> sacks the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1430</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> captures the city from the Venetians</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1519</span><span class="rf-text">Ottoman archives record a Jewish majority of 54%</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1912</span><span class="rf-text">The Ottoman garrison surrenders to the Greek army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">A fire destroys most of the old centre and leaves 72,000 people homeless</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1943</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> begins</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cassander"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cassander.jpg" alt="Cassander" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kassander316BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Megistias, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thessaloniki lies on the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a>, at the north-west corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, and the delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a> bounds it on the west. The city was founded around 315 BCE by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, on or near the site of the ancient town of Therma and 26 other local villages. He named it after his wife Thessalonike, a half-sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and a princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> as daughter of Philip II. Her name means "Thessalian victory" and honours a Macedonian victory at the Battle of Crocus Field in 353 or 352 BCE. A popular Greek legend holds that Thessalonike became a mermaid who lived in the Aegean after Alexander's death.</p><p>The city was built 40 km south-east of Pella, the capital of the Kingdom of Macedonia, and Pella remained the capital until the kingdom fell. Under the kingdom Thessaloniki kept its own autonomy and a parliament of its own, and it became the most important city in Macedonia. It had its first walls, enclosing and protecting the city, as early as the 2nd century BCE. The name Saloniki is first attested in Greek in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> in the 14th century, and the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> already called the city Salunik in the 12th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/galerius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galerius.jpg" alt="Galerius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romuliana_Galerius_head.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shinjirod, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> Thessaloniki grew into an important trade hub on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>, the road that linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and carried trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The Romans built a spacious harbour there, the Burrowed Harbour, which served the city's trade until the 18th century, and silt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a> later allowed land to be reclaimed and the port to be extended. The city also lay at the southern end of the main north to south route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, along the valleys of the Morava and the Axios. Under the tetrarchy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> had an imperial palace, a new hippodrome, a triumphal arch and a mausoleum built there.</p><p>From the first years of the Byzantine Empire Thessaloniki was counted as its second city after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in wealth and in size, with a population of 150,000 in the mid-12th century. In the 14th century its population is put at 100,000 to 150,000, which made it larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> at that time. Under the Komnenoi its economy expanded, and the city housed an imperial mint and held the fair of St Demetrius each October outside its walls. In 1519 it was both the largest Jewish city in the world and the only one with a Jewish majority. After 1870 the population rose by 70%, and the railway reached the city in 1888.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-antony.jpg" alt="Mark Antony" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octavian_and_Antony_denarius_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Byzantium565, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> fell, Thessaloniki passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 128 BCE it became the capital of the Roman province of Macedonia, and in 41 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> made it a free city. Under the tetrarchy it was the administrative capital of one of the four portions of the empire, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a>. In 379, when the Prefecture of Illyricum was divided between the eastern and western empires, it became the capital of the new prefecture, and by the fall of Rome in 476 it was the second-largest city of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, and Thessaloniki became the seat of the Kingdom of Thessalonica, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>. In 1224 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> overran it and made it the capital of the Empire of Thessalonica, and after his defeat at Klokotnitsa in 1230 the city became a vassal of the Second Bulgarian Empire until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> took it in 1246. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it after a siege of 1383 to 1387, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> recovered it in 1403. Despot Andronikos Palaiologos ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1423, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> captured it on 29 March 1430.</p><p>The Ottomans held it for nearly five centuries as the capital of the Sanjak of Selanik, later of the Selanik Eyalet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a>. It passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in November 1912, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of 1913 confirmed this.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle.jpg" alt="Paul the Apostle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_of_Saint_Paul_at_Ephesus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thessaloniki was among the early centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. About 50 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> visited the city's chief synagogue on three Sabbaths, and he later wrote two letters to the new church there, which appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> as First and Second Thessalonians. In 306 the city acquired a patron saint, St Demetrius, and a basilica in his name was first built in the fifth century. The brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> were natives of the city, and in the ninth century they created the first literary language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, Old Church Slavonic, most likely from the Slavic dialect of the hinterland of their home town.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> the Muslim population grew substantially. The 1478 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 6,094 Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> households and 4,320 Muslim ones, and no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, but soon after 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> arrived, following their expulsion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under the Alhambra Decree of 1492. Some historians consider that the Ottoman invitation to Jewish settlement was meant to prevent the Christians from dominating the city. Most of the city's tradespeople were Jewish, and the Jewish community spoke Judeo-Spanish, a dialect of Spanish. In 1890 the population was 118,000, of whom 47% were Jews, 22% Turks, 14% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and 8% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>; in 1913 it was 157,889, with Jews at 39%, Turks 29% and Greeks 25%. The walls were torn down between 1869 and 1889, the first tram ran in 1888, and electric street lighting came in 1908.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-i.jpg" alt="Theodosius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Theodosius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">www.livius.org, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 390 the city rose in revolt against the detention of a favourite charioteer, and troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> carried out a massacre of its inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> besieged the city several times in the sixth and seventh centuries without taking it, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miracles-of-saint-demetrius">Miracles of Saint Demetrius</a> narrate. A naval force led by Leo of Tripoli sacked it in 904, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> sacked it again in 1185. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> took it in 1430, contemporary reports estimated that about one fifth of the population was enslaved. In May 1821, at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>, the governor Yusuf Bey had half of more than 400 hostages killed in front of him.</p><p>The Great Fire began on 18 August 1917 from an unattended kitchen fire and burned for 32 hours. It destroyed most of the old centre, two churches and many synagogues and mosques, and left 72,000 people homeless, 50,000 of them Jewish according to the Pallis Report, of a population of about 271,157. The government forbade quick rebuilding so that a new plan, headed by the French architect Ernest Hebrard, could be carried out.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied the city in April 1941. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were forced into a ghetto, and from 15 March 1943 about 45,000 were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> and Bergen-Belsen; most were murdered at once, and only 4% of those sent to Auschwitz survived. The earthquakes of June 1978 damaged buildings and monuments, and an apartment building collapsed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Several early Christian and Byzantine monuments of Thessaloniki were added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage list in 1988, and Byzantine mosaics survive in some of its churches, including the Hagia Sophia and the church of St George. The Arch and Tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> also remain. Of the more than 40 minarets of the Ottoman city, most were demolished after 1912 or fell in the fire of 1917, and the only one that survives stands at the Rotonda, which is part of the Arch and Tomb of Galerius. The White Tower, left standing when the old walls were taken down, is the city's main landmark. The Ano Poli, the upper town, escaped the fire of 1917 and keeps its Ottoman and Greek houses, its Byzantine fort the Heptapyrgion and part of the walls.</p><p>The plan drawn up after 1917 gave the centre its wide diagonal avenues and monumental squares. The city is also called the Symprotevousa, the "co-capital", after its place beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and in 1916 it was the seat of the Provisional Government of National Defence under Venizelos. Atatürk was born there in 1881, and the Young Turks began their revolution of 1908 from the city. The Greek exchange of populations after 1922 brought over 160,000 Greek refugees, and the Jewish population fell to a minority for the first time since the 16th century.</p><p>The municipality had 319,045 inhabitants in 2021, and the metropolitan area about one million.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Thessaloniki, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a>, was founded around 315 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> and became a Roman provincial capital and the second city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Ottoman rule from 1430 made it a largely Jewish city; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> took it in 1912, and today it is the second-largest city of Greece.</em></p>

